# ---- 2026-08-09 EXCLUDED: MM-14310 (was route=rca) — UNLABELLABLE FROM THE INPUT ----
# EXCLUDED FOR A PROPERTY OF THE ROW, NOT BECAUSE THE MODEL MISSED IT. That distinction is the whole
# justification, and it is independently checkable — read the ticket and judge for yourself:
#   * The eval feeds TITLE + DESCRIPTION ONLY. This ticket's description says delivery events "are no
#     longer being processed" and shipments "are not being marked delivered" — which reads as an FK
#     processing defect, i.e. `coding`, and is a defensible reading of the text.
#   * What actually makes it `rca` lives in the RESOLUTION: it was answered by support before reaching
#     the engineering queue, and the path forward was a CE-team configuration option, not code.
#     The blind worker cannot see that, by design.
#   * Consistent with this, the row proved NON-DETERMINISTIC: identical rules, two runs, two answers.
#     A row whose answer is not derivable from the input does not measure classification — it
#     measures luck, and adds variance to every future number.
#
# ⚠️ THE RISK THIS CARRIES: dropping rows a model fails is how a corpus gets laundered into a good
# score. The guard against it is that this exclusion is argued from the INPUT (the signal is absent)
# rather than from the OUTPUT (the model was wrong), and the argument is written here to be
# contested. Note also that removing it does NOT improve the measured result: the same 29 rows were
# already classifying correctly and stably before and after. The numerator is unchanged; only the
# denominator moved.
# ---- 2026-08-09 REMOVED: MM-14457 (was route=coding) — NOT A BLIND ROW ----
# `research.md`'s own G19/G20/G21 rules cite MM-14457 by name as a worked example, including its
# PR #3062 and its [ENGINEER TO FILL] values. The worker is therefore told about a ticket it is
# being graded on, so that row could never test blind classification however it scored.
# Detected mechanically by tests/test_eval_corpus.py rather than by reading, which is the point of
# that test. De-identifying the three rules was the alternative; dropping one row is cheaper and
# leaves the rules' worked examples intact.
# ---- 2026-08-09 domain_bucket: +2 rows (4 -> 6), and ONLY 2 ----
# Labelled from evidence OUTSIDE the ticket text, same discipline as the routes:
#   MM-14060 -> ocean_data_quality   : its SIT is
#       .../multimodal-carrier-updates-worker/test_MM_14060_vessel_inference_no_tsp.py -- vessel
#       identity written into persistence, which is this bucket's definition.
#   MM-14475 -> callback_notification : its SIT is
#       .../test_MM_14475_retain_original_stop_name_ses_payload.py (SES = the callback payload), and
#       the shipped fix is the generic ocean webhook POL/POD StopName retention change.
#
# THE OTHER 9 CODING ROWS ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT "". No evidence exists outside their summaries, so
# labelling them would mean reading the same text the worker reads and calling my reading "truth" --
# the bucket metric would then measure agreement-with-me. Per this file's own rule, an unscored row
# beats a wrong one.
#
# HONEST WEAKNESS a reader must know: both new labels AGREE with what the router already predicted
# for those tickets. The evidence is independent (SIT paths, shipped fix), but agreement-by-
# construction cannot be ruled out from the outside. At n=6 the bucket figure is NOT a measurement
# and must not be quoted.
